  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/5a7c024edcbc713b2b7942d024c0d8c4502e1283526ef6a245849d6cd637a84b.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/50154c0711cd6fdb23714ee78d1e7e88253ffd7cf5d100f2f3fdf11ac4a00908.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/7b4f0ced2e2f4ae917f82fbc956916d14a6070f6d20f0c2eab06566f91e7fe66.png)\n\nThe point $(2,6)$ is at a distance of $6$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(2,3)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $6-3=3.$",
